## openshift-intro

oc -- cli managing openshift cluster (kubectl has a subset of commands)

# login 
oc login -u username -p password https://endpoint.rednat.com:port
- context sotred in .kube file in homedir

# node represents a host in kubernetes 
- (node roles: master - dedicated to decision making in cluster - assign tasks to worker nodes, master makes sure the cluster state corresponds to configuration / worker - run user workloads) / control-plane (controls config and infra)
- node is a coreos rhel (immutable, doesn't use packages but an image tree -- like e.g. android)
- master nodes run buch of containers, e.g.: etcd -- database that is the source of config, openshift-kube-shceduler -- schedules and runs containers on nodes -- using proces kubelet services that runs on all hosts
- systemct status kubelet -- the service instructs the crio service (container engine)
- *resource* -- unit of configuration of cluster, declarative style

# oc command style
oc <verb> <resource-type> <instance-node>(optional - omit to get all nodes)

oc get node

# run a container on openshift
pod - a shared resource for one or more containers (but mostly one container per pod), provides ipaddr, memory, etc.

oc run myhttpd --image registry.access.redhat.com/ubi/httpd-24 --dry-run > pod.yaml

(oc creates config for the pod, sends it to opensift via api) 
- config contains info on what config is created (kind: Pod), metadata and spec (that depend on kind)
- all configuration of oc is done via specification documents
- apply existing config:

oc apply -f pod.yml

# service resource
- makes a pod available to the os cluster using a stable ip address (pod ips are volatile)
- internally it uses a haproxy
- ip is cluster-wide -- not available outside cluser -- you need route resource for that
- object desc:

oc get pod -o wide

oc expose pod/myhttpd --port 8080 --dry-run=client -o yaml > service.yaml
- create a service
oc get service 
os describe service myhttpd
- shows endpoint -- points to pod

# project -- a name space for pods
oc new-project name

# route resource
- routes use services to reach the pod
- only for http, https
- sort of mapping between outside world and a service

oc expose svc/myhttpd --dry-run=client -o yaml  > route.yml
oc get routes -- shows a fqdn

oc get all
- shows pod > service > route
- request goes from the route fqdn > service > pod

# deployment resource
- pod is an immutable resource
- we can pass parameters to it via env variables (env: in pod spec yaml), but need to recreate the spec and re-deploy
- deployment contains a template for pods
- deployment is not immutable

oc create deployment myttpd --image <container-image> --dry-run -o yaml > dry.run 
- creates a pod as well

oc rhs pod/<podname> -- remote shell to pod
oc edit deployment/myttpd
- change env vars for pods in deployment

oc get pods --labels
- conection betw service and pod is defined by labels, see service spec...
- or expose the deployment -- oc does the connection automatically, non need to edit specs 

# replicaset resource
-- manages how many replicas of the app should be there
-- configurable by the deployment resource

oc scale deployment httpd --count 3

# the opensift way
oc new-app <image>
-- does all the above, creates resources in one go and extends it with some more resources (openshift functionality)
-- sets ports based on image metadata

